WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.714
Name the game station’s purpose before planning the rotation.

2
00:00:03.914 --> 00:00:09.322
Is it typing accuracy, vocabulary application or explanation of a strategy?

3
00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:13.308
Use a short interval and describe what the learner will monitor.

4
00:00:13.508 --> 00:00:18.837
Provide a response route that does not depend on fast typing, sound or a headset.

5
00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:24.915
A calm paper puzzle, oral response or partner input can preserve the same learning demand.

6
00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:29.729
Compare a learner’s progress with their own earlier performance when that is appropriate.

7
00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:34.024
Do not assume that a slower typist knows less subject content.

8
00:00:34.224 --> 00:00:40.667
Preview the text, sound, motion, themes and controls and follow local expectations.

9
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:46.593
The age-band suggestions in the field guide are teaching recommendations, not formal ratings.

10
00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:51.335
Let students pause and choose an equivalent practice route when needed.

11
00:00:51.535 --> 00:00:57.578
End with a brief individual debrief: what did you try, what happened and what will you change?

12
00:00:57.778 --> 00:01:02.312
Then ask a fresh content or strategy task without the game’s cues.

13
00:01:02.512 --> 00:01:07.321
Use that response to decide whether the activity supported the intended learning.

14
00:01:07.521 --> 00:01:15.053
Pause and write your station card with its purpose, timing, control instructions, alternative and debrief question.

